The frontend foundation is in good shape: I found 17 HTML pages, all local links and assets resolve, every page has a title, description, viewport setting, one <h1>, image alt text, and labelled form inputs.
Before we discuss a backend, these are the important things to finish or decide.
Must fix before launch
1. Remove all demo authentication before real users arrive.
   [main.js](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators\assets\js\main.js:1) lets anyone “log in” with any email/password and saves identity information in localStorage. This is okay for a prototype, but it is not secure authentication. Anyone can alter browser storage or bypass the dashboard redirect.
   Do not put protected resources or real member information in [member-dashboard.html](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators\pages\member-dashboard.html:1) until real authentication is connected.
2. Do not launch the Join, Donate, Contact, or Newsletter forms as if they work.
   Their demonstration notices are good, but the forms currently do not send anything. Before launch, connect:
   - Join → membership system + hosted payment checkout
   - Login → real identity/authentication provider
   - Donate → Stripe, PayPal, or donation/CRM hosted checkout
   - Contact/newsletter → CRM or email form provider
   Never process card details, passwords, or membership data in browser-only JavaScript.
3. Replace all sample and placeholder content.
   This includes sample pricing, events, articles, testimonials, contact details, impact figures, videos, and “coming soon” text. Examples: [membership.html](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators\pages\membership.html:1), [donate.html](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators\pages\donate.html:1), and [what_new.html](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators\pages\what_new.html:1).
4. Verify organisation claims.
   Before publishing, someone authorised by the organisation should approve claims about:
   - nonprofit / 501(c)(3) status;
   - tax deductibility;
   - membership benefits and legal/professional protection;
   - global availability;
   - response times and support promises;
   - statements of faith and privacy policy.
Important project improvements
- Add a [.gitignore](C:\Users\USER PC\christian_educators.gitignore) before backend work. It should later exclude .env, credentials, database files, logs, and build output. There is currently no .gitignore.
- Add a README.md documenting how to run the site, deploy it, and where real service credentials belong.
- Add automated checks: HTML validation, broken-link checking, accessibility checks, and mobile browser testing.
- Add social sharing metadata—Open Graph image/title/description—before public launch.
- Create robots.txt and sitemap.xml when you have the final domain.
- Use HTTPS only in deployment, and configure security headers later: Content Security Policy, HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options, and clickjacking protection.
- Do not publish the .git directory through your web host.
What is already safe
- No API keys, secrets, payment keys, or external third-party scripts were found.
- No external URLs were found in the project, so there are no unreviewed external embeds yet.
- No broken local links were found; the membership links using ?tier=... are valid—the query string only selects a membership tier.
The next decision before backend development is: do you want a managed membership platform to handle joining, login, renewals, payments, and member data—or a custom backend? For this kind of organisation, a managed platform is usually safer and faster.